     6	"""xcode-mcp-front daemon.
     7	
     8	Problem: Xcode's MCP-connection "Allow" prompt is keyed per connecting PID. Every
     9	separate process that execs `xcrun mcpbridge` (one per kicker node, one per Claude
    10	Code / Codex session) makes Xcode ask again. kicker runs many concurrent nodes, so
    11	that's many concurrent popups for the same underlying tool.
    12	
    13	Fix: hold exactly ONE `xcrun mcpbridge` child process for the whole life of this
    14	daemon, and front it with a Streamable HTTP MCP server. Every downstream client
    15	(kicker nodes, Claude Code, Codex — anything that can speak MCP-over-HTTP) points
    16	at this daemon's URL instead of spawning mcpbridge itself. Xcode approves ONE PID,
    17	once, and stays approved as long as this daemon (and its mcpbridge child) keep running.
    18	
    19	Runs in one of two modes, chosen by which env vars are set (see below):
    20	  - SINGLE-upstream (default, what's been running since 2026-08-14): fronts just
    21	    one upstream (normally mcpbridge). Dumb passthrough — tool names forwarded
    22	    unprefixed, exactly as the upstream advertises them. This is the deployed
    23	    xcode-mcp-front daemon; nothing about its behavior changes in this mode.
    24	  - MULTI-upstream (XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAMS set): fronts several upstreams
    25	    behind the SAME endpoint, tool names prefixed per-upstream
    26	    (`<name>__<tool>`, e.g. `xcode__BuildProject`, `drews__run_project_unmonitored`)
    27	    so two upstreams that happen to share a tool name can't collide or shadow
    28	    each other. Built for exactly one reason: Drew's xcode-mcp
    29	    (`drews-xcode-mcp`) has no approval-dialog friction on its own — a client can
    30	    already spawn `uvx drews-xcode-mcp` directly with zero setup, no wrapper
    31	    needed — so it never got its own standalone daemon (built, verified, then
    32	    torn back down, 2026-08-14 — see git history). But "one endpoint with BOTH
    33	    Xcode tools available, prefixed so there's no confusion" is a real,
    34	    different thing worth having alongside the single-upstream daemon, not
    35	    instead of it. Both modes share every line of connection/click/reconnect
    36	    logic below via the Upstream class — no duplicated logic between them.
    37	
    38	This is a dumb passthrough, not a real aggregator: it forwards list_tools/call_tool
    39	to the relevant upstream ClientSession(s) and returns whatever comes back. It does
    40	not interpret tool semantics. tools/call requests to a given upstream are
    41	serialized through that upstream's own lock; tools/list requests — client drains
    42	and the heartbeat alike — run WITHOUT the lock on a session snapshot, because the
    43	MCP session multiplexes concurrent requests by id and a list must never wait
    44	behind a long-running build (measured by the phase-1 panel: a client connecting
    45	during one upstream's slow call got no tool list at all until it finished).
    46	Different upstreams are fully independent of each other.
    47	
    48	VALIDATED 2026-08-14 (all live, against a real Xcode):
    49	- One connection tolerates many sequential calls of different shapes, no reconnect
    50	  needed (spike.py, then a real multi-call session through this daemon).
    51	- Quitting Xcode does NOT crash this process — it keeps serving HTTP, but the
    52	  mcpbridge child dies and every call after that fails clean with "Connection
    53	  closed".
    54	- The approval prompt's PID (confirmed from the actual dialog, not assumed) is
    55	  THIS daemon's own PID, not the ephemeral mcpbridge child's — so as long as this
    56	  process keeps running, reconnecting mcpbridge in a loop should NOT need a fresh
    57	  approval each time, only the very first connection (or after a genuine
    58	  permission reset) does.
    59	- The approval prompt is requested LAZILY, on the first list_tools call, not at
    60	  the initial connect — the per-upstream reconnect loop polls for it (and
    61	  clicks it) continuously in the connected steady-state too, not just while
    62	  reconnecting.
    63	- Xcode's approval dialogs do NOT stack — only one shows at a time, and a stale
    64	  unanswered one blocks the next (including this daemon's own) from appearing.
    65	  The click logic reads each dialog's own PID: clicks Allow for our own live
    66	  pid, clicks Don't Allow for a dead pid (nobody's waiting on it — a plain
    67	  os.kill(pid,0) liveness check, no PID-history file needed), leaves any other
    68	  live pid's dialog strictly alone.
    69	- Drew's xcode-mcp (drews-xcode-mcp) has NO approval-dialog behavior at all —
    70	  folder-allowlist auth (XCODEMCP_ALLOWED_FOLDERS / --allowed) instead of a
    71	  live per-PID popup. Its Upstream instance just never finds a dialog to click;
    72	  harmless no-op, no special-casing needed.
    73	
    74	Env overrides — SINGLE-upstream mode (default):
    75	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAM_CMD    default "xcrun"
    76	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAM_ARGS   default "mcpbridge" (space-separated)
    77	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_REQUIRE_XCODE   default "1" — wait for Xcode.app before connecting.
    78	                                   Set "0" for an upstream (like drews-xcode-mcp)
    79	                                   that doesn't need a live Xcode process at all.
    80	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_AUTO_ALLOW      default "1" — click-Allow-automatically. Off
