   276	# That is why a defect in this function is not cosmetic. It is the only path to a working
   277	# connection, so a branch that silently declines to click takes the whole daemon down while
   278	# every log line still says "connected".
   279	
   280	
   281	# How long another process's approval dialog is left alone before we clear it. Only once we
   282	# have been blocked for this long is it fair to assume nobody is coming to answer it.
   283	# HOW LONG A FOREIGN DIALOG MAY BLOCK US BEFORE WE CLEAR IT. This MUST be comfortably less
   284	# than CONNECT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS or two daemons deadlock by arithmetic rather than by bad luck.
   285	#
   286	# It was 45 against a 15-second connect timeout. Xcode's approval dialogs do not stack, so when
   287	# both front daemons are up, each one's clicker finds the other's dialog, correctly classifies it
   288	# as foreign-and-alive, and waits out a grace period three times longer than the connection
   289	# attempt it is holding up. Its own prompt therefore never surfaces, its list_tools times out,
