1. **High: A valid single-server file config with an empty prefix bypasses both sieve and map enforcement.** I read that `_check_prefixes()` permits an empty prefix when only one server exists ([mcp_config.py:265](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:265)). `build_server()` then classifies any such server as passthrough ([daemon.py:848](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:848)), and a dispatch miss goes directly to `call_tool()` without checking blocks or maps ([daemon.py:1030](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:1030)). A file config containing `prefix: ""`, a blocked `secret`, and a mapped `read` will hide or rename them in `tools/list`, but direct calls to `secret` and the old `read` name still reach the upstream. Restrict passthrough to the legacy environment-created spec, or route every file-configured server through the normal block, refresh, and dispatch path.

2. **High: Call-time refresh can overwrite another upstream’s valid dispatch entry.** The full composition path checks collisions and degrades aliases ([daemon.py:933](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:933)), but `_refresh_upstream_dispatch()` assigns each name without collision checks and without removing that upstream’s previous entries ([daemon.py:979](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:979), [daemon.py:991](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:991)). Suppose `beta__ping` belongs naturally to beta, while alpha maps `old_ping` to `beta__ping`. A full list can preserve beta and degrade alpha, but any later dispatch miss that refreshes alpha overwrites `beta__ping`; subsequent calls route to alpha. Use one composition function for full lists and refreshes. Replace an upstream’s entries transactionally and apply the same global collision policy before publishing them.

3. **High: Dynamic alias collision handling depends on server order and can remove the wrong tool.** The loop processes upstreams in config order ([daemon.py:905](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:905)). It degrades a colliding alias only when the mapped tool is processed second ([daemon.py:933](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:933)); when the alias is processed first, a later natural prefixed tool is treated as an ordinary duplicate and dropped ([daemon.py:944](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:944)). Reversing two servers therefore changes which implementation owns the exposed name. First reserve every nonblocked natural prefixed name, then admit aliases that do not collide with that reserved surface.

4. **High: A duplicated mapped tool resurrects its replaced original name.** When a mapped exposed name already exists, the code assumes an alias collision and falls back to `prefix + bare` ([daemon.py:933](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:933), [daemon.py:941](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:941)). If pagination or a mid-drain mutation returns the same mapped tool twice, the first copy publishes the alias and the second publishes the prefixed original. Both names then route, contrary to replacement semantics. Compare the existing dispatch target with `(u, bare)` before collision degradation; drop an identical duplicate instead of creating a fallback.

5. **Medium: The description matcher is not an exact-reference matcher for overlapping names or punctuation at an edge.** I read that alternatives retain config order inside one `\b(...)\b` expression ([daemon.py:496](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:496)). A direct probe showed that maps ordered as `foo`, then `foo-bar` rewrite `use foo-bar` as `use <mapped-foo>-bar`; reversing the order rewrites the full name. The same defect occurs with `foo` and `foo.bar`. Names such as `.foo`, `foo.`, and `-foo-` never match at all. Replace exact names longest-first and define boundaries using the permitted tool-name character set, rather than `\b`.

6. **Medium: Description rewriting uses configured aliases after collision degradation has dropped those aliases.** `rewrite_description()` always substitutes the configured exposed name ([daemon.py:509](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:509)). The composition loop performs that rewrite before it detects a collision and changes the tool to its prefixed fallback ([daemon.py:924](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:924), [daemon.py:941](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:941)). A sibling description can therefore instruct the model to call an alias that now belongs to another upstream. Resolve global names first, construct the effective old-to-published-name table, and rewrite descriptions from that table afterward.

7. **Medium: Strict `why` enforcement is available but is not connected to an authoring or installation gate.** The validator defaults to strict loading ([mcp_config.py:207](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:207)), while every daemon file load explicitly uses lenient mode ([mcp_config.py:304](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:304)). The current launcher writes `_mcp_info.json` and immediately starts the daemon without running the validator ([xcode-combined-front-run.sh:29](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/xcode-combined-front-run.sh:29), [xcode-combined-front-run.sh:42](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/xcode-combined-front-run.sh:42)); the template installer also delegates and records members without a config-validation step ([template.py:228](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/lib/template.py:228)). I infer that a generated template can ship without `why`, leaving the lenient daemon warning as the only enforcement. Add strict validation to the template source test and installation transaction before the generated file becomes active.

8. **Medium: Dispatch publication has no generation control across concurrent lists and refreshes.** Each downstream list builds a snapshot and then clears and replaces the shared dispatch table ([daemon.py:971](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:971)); refresh mutates the same table after an awaited upstream request ([daemon.py:982](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:982)). A slower request based on an older catalogue can complete after a newer request and roll dispatch backward. Phase 4 notifications increase the chance of this interleaving. Serialize dispatch publication or attach catalogue generations and refuse to publish an older generation.

9. **Low: The tests cover the normal paths but cannot expose the defects above.** Every daemon call test occurs after an initial list ([test-mcp-front-daemon.sh:73](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/tests/test-mcp-front-daemon.sh:73)), so static-map lookup before the first list is untested. There is no empty-prefix policy test, collision test in both server orders, duplicate mapped-tool test, mutable catalogue, or concurrent list/refresh test. The stub stores tools in a dictionary, which eliminates duplicate names ([stub_mcp_server.py:50](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/tests/stub_mcp_server.py:50)), and it processes requests serially ([stub_mcp_server.py:76](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/tests/stub_mcp_server.py:76)). Add a mutable, concurrent stub mode that can change catalogues between pages and emit `notifications/tools/list_changed`.

Verified non-findings:

- Outside the empty-prefix passthrough, blocked tools are removed during full composition and refresh, and dispatch misses return the recorded reason ([daemon.py:915](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:915), [daemon.py:987](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:987), [daemon.py:1039](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:1039)).
- Static contradictions are rejected correctly: one tool cannot be blocked and mapped, duplicate mapped names within one server fail, and two servers cannot statically claim the same alias ([mcp_config.py:110](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:110), [mcp_config.py:159](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:159), [mcp_config.py:243](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:243)).
- Ordinary `foo.bar` and `foo-bar` names rewrite correctly when no shorter mapped name overlaps them. The defect is overlap ordering and punctuation at the outer edge.
- The static-map scan runs only on a dispatch miss, not on every call ([daemon.py:1002](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:1002)). Its linear cost is secondary to its collision behavior.
- Explicit description overrides correctly win without mechanical mutation, and old-name references produce a warning as the roadmap requires ([daemon.py:924](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:924)).
- Unavailable upstreams are checked before policy application, and a disconnected dispatch target cannot reach its old session because `call_tool()` checks both session presence and `known_broken` ([daemon.py:906](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:906), [daemon.py:597](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:597)).
- I found no tautological Phase 2–3 assertion. The shared RED helper requires both the exact nonzero exit code and a literal diagnostic ([lib.sh:53](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/tests/lib.sh:53)).

The read-only sandbox prevented both shell suites from creating their `mktemp` directories, so those executions did not yield code results. The regex and empty-prefix configuration probes ran without filesystem writes.
